course: dictionary
lesson: 7019
title: One word, many places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the clues in the sentence tell you which one is meant. «They leave him with a porter at the station, catch a slow train home, hand Toad to his housekeeper and row back to their own cosy parlour.» Cosy and home tell you that a parlour here is a room in a house.
explain: In old books, the parlour is the best room of a house, kept for visitors or for the family to relax in. «How jolly a fire of logs in the parlour would be» shows a warm, comfortable room, the sort of room where a family sits by the fire.
explain: Look at the words next to parlour. «An ice-cream parlour» is a shop, and «a milking parlour» is a building on a farm where cows are milked. The word before parlour often tells you what kind of place it is.
fact: A parlour can be a room in a house, a shop or business, or a building on a farm.
fact: «Parlour» in old books means a sitting room kept for visitors or for the family to relax in.
fact: An ice-cream parlour or a beauty parlour is a shop that gives a certain service.
fact: A funeral parlour is where the body of a person who has died is looked after before a funeral.
fact: A milking parlour is the building on a farm where cows are milked.
fact: A parlour game is an indoor party game, like charades.
exercise: «They row back to their own cosy parlour.» In this sentence, what is a parlour? | A shop that sells ice cream | *A room in a house, for sitting and relaxing | A boat for rowing across the river
exercise: What is a milking parlour? | A shop for buying milk in bottles | *A building on a farm where cows are milked | A room for playing party games
exercise: Mia visits an ice-cream parlour. What kind of place is it? | *A shop that gives a certain service, here selling ice cream | A building where people are looked after before a funeral | A room in an old house for visitors
test: What is a beauty parlour? | A farm building for milking cows | A room in an old house for visitors | *A shop that gives a service such as hair and make-up | A party game played indoors
test: «There was panic in the parlours and howling in the halls.» What does parlours most likely mean in this line? | *Rooms in a house | Shops that sell sweets | A cow-shed on a farm | Boats on the river
exam: Jayden says a funeral parlour is a fun place for a party. Is he right? | Yes, it is a shop selling ice cream | *No, it is where the body of a person who has died is looked after before a funeral | No, it is a farm building for cows | Yes, it is a place for indoor party games
